The canonical product hierarchy is:

```text
OmniSight Platform
│
└── Super Admin
    │
    ├── Organizations
    │   │
    │   ├── Service Model
    │   │   ├── MANAGED
    │   │   └── CUSTOMER_DB
    │   │
    │   ├── Subscription
    │   │   ├── Package / Plan
    │   │   ├── Payment
    │   │   ├── Invoice
    │   │   ├── Start Date
    │   │   ├── End Date
    │   │   └── Status
    │   │
    │   ├── Organization Admin
    │   ├── Manager
    │   ├── Viewer
    │   ├── Employees
    │   ├── Universal Agents / Devices
    │   └── Organization Data
    │
    └── Platform Configuration
        ├── Packages
        ├── Pricing
        ├── Landing Page
        ├── Branding
        ├── Business Settings
        ├── Subscription Rules
        ├── Platform Policies
        └── Platform Audit
```

---

# 4. Product Principles

OmniSight must follow these principles.

## 4.1 Super Admin Authority

The Super Admin is the platform owner and highest authority.

The Super Admin controls:

* Organizations
* Organization status
* Organization service model
* Packages/plans
* Pricing
* Subscriptions
* Payments
* Invoices
* Subscription duration
* Organization provisioning
* Organization Admin provisioning
* Agent provisioning
* Agent issuance/revocation
* Platform settings
* Landing page business configuration
* Branding
* Platform-level policies
* Platform audit information
* Organization suspension/resumption
* Customer infrastructure approval
* Infrastructure migration approval
* Service-model migration approval

No Organization Admin can override Super Admin-owned platform, commercial, subscription, or deployment decisions.

---

# 5. Multi-Tenant Architecture

OmniSight must be a strict multi-tenant system.

Each customer organization is an independent tenant.

Organization A must never be able to access:

* Organization B users
* Organization B employees
* Organization B agents
* Organization B screenshots
* Organization B activity
* Organization B locations
* Organization B reports
* Organization B projects
* Organization B settings
* Organization B subscription data
* Organization B audit data
* Organization B customer infrastructure configuration
* Organization B storage objects
* Organization B database records

Tenant isolation must be enforced server-side.

Client-side filtering is not considered sufficient security.

Every organization-scoped request must establish organization context from authenticated server-side identity.

The server must never trust a client-provided `organizationId` as the sole basis for authorization.

---

# 6. Platform Data vs Organization Data

OmniSight must explicitly distinguish between:

```text
Platform Data
```

and:

```text
Organization Data
```

## 6.1 Platform Data

Platform-owned data may include:

* Super Admin identity
* Organization registry
* Platform configuration
* Packages
* Pricing
* Global commercial configuration
* Platform branding
* Landing page configuration
* Platform policies
* Platform-level audit information
* Global service configuration
* Global Agent protocol information
* Platform operational metadata

Platform data remains under OmniSight platform control.

## 6.2 Organization Data

Organization-owned data may include:

* Organization users
* Employees
* Agents
* Devices
* Activity
* Locations
* Screenshots
* Monitoring records
* Screenshot metadata
* Organization operational settings
* Organization policies
* Organization reports
* Organization-specific operational history

Organization data must be isolated by tenant.

The exact placement of organization data depends on the active Service Model.

---

# 7. User Roles

## 7.1 Super Admin

The Super Admin is the OmniSight platform owner.

Capabilities include:

* Create Organization
* Edit Organization
* Activate Organization
* Pause Organization
* Resume Organization
* Manage subscription
* Manage package
* Record payment
* Record invoice
* Set subscription dates
* Set service model
* Provision Organization Admin
* Manage organization users when necessary
* View organization operational status
* View Agent/device status
* Configure platform policies
* Configure landing page
* Configure pricing
* Configure branding
* View platform audit logs
* Manage platform settings
* Build/generate Agent packages
* Approve infrastructure changes
* Reject infrastructure changes
* Approve migration
* Monitor migration
* Perform controlled cutover
* Initiate rollback where applicable
* Revoke Agents

Super Admin has cross-organization visibility.

---

# 8. Organization Roles

Each organization may contain:

```text
ORGANIZATION_ADMIN
MANAGER
VIEWER
EMPLOYEE
```

---

# 9. Organization Admin

The Organization Admin manages the customer's organization.

Organization Admin may:

* Manage organization users
* Create employees/users according to allowed rules
* Assign Manager role
* Assign Viewer role
* Manage organization-level operational settings
* View organization subscription information
* View service model
* View package
* View subscription duration
* View subscription status
* View relevant operational data
* Manage organization-controlled features where permitted
* Submit customer database configuration
* Submit customer storage configuration
* Request infrastructure change
* View infrastructure validation status
* View migration status

Organization Admin must NOT:

* independently change service model
* independently activate a new service model
* approve infrastructure migration
* change package price
* change subscription price
* alter payment records
* extend subscription without Super Admin authorization
* change platform branding
* change platform business configuration
* access another organization
* access another organization's database
* access another organization's storage
* bypass server-side permissions
* bypass Super Admin approval

---

# 10. Manager

Manager is an operational role.

The exact feature permissions must be permission-driven rather than hard-coded throughout the UI.

Typical capabilities may include:

* View assigned employees
* View activity
* View monitoring information
* View screenshots where permitted
* View operational reports
* Manage assigned operational resources
* Perform allowed employee/project/task operations

Manager must not automatically receive Organization Admin privileges.

---

# 11. Viewer

Viewer is a read-only operational role.

Viewer may access data explicitly permitted by the organization's permission policy.

Viewer must not:

* modify organization configuration
* manage users
* modify subscription
* modify payment
* change service model
* modify platform configuration
* perform privileged administrative actions

---

# 12. Employee

Employee is an organization-scoped operational identity.

Employee permissions must be explicitly defined by the permission model.

Employee must never receive administrative privileges merely because an Employee record exists.

Employee access must remain organization-scoped.

---

# 13. Service Models — V1 Active

OmniSight V1 has exactly two official customer-facing service models:

```text
1. MANAGED
2. CUSTOMER_DB
```

Human-readable names:

```text
OmniSight Managed
Customer Database
```

Private/self-hosted deployment is NOT a V1 service model.

Private deployment must not be:

* selectable
* exposed
* offered
* provisioned
* generated by Agent Builder
* exposed through customer-facing APIs
* represented as an active V1 deployment option

Future private/self-hosted architecture may be considered later.

---

# 14. Universal Agent Architecture

OmniSight uses ONE Universal Agent architecture.

There must not be separate production Agent variants such as:

```text
Managed Agent
Customer DB Agent
Private Agent
```

The canonical Agent is:

```text
Universal OmniSight Agent
```

The same Agent architecture must work with both V1 service models.

The Agent is OmniSight-centric.

The Agent primarily needs to know:

* OmniSight Server/API URL
* Agent protocol version
* Agent version
* secure device identity
* secure enrollment/authentication credential
* server-authorized runtime configuration

The Agent must NOT contain customer infrastructure knowledge.

The Agent must NOT contain:

* customer database username/password
* customer database connection secrets
* customer database host credentials
* customer storage access keys
* customer storage secret keys
* customer storage passwords
* customer infrastructure credentials
* hard-coded customer database endpoints
* hard-coded customer storage endpoints

The Agent communicates with OmniSight.

OmniSight determines the organization's destination.

---

# 15. Universal Agent Data Flow

The canonical architecture is:

```text
Universal Agent
       ↓
OmniSight API
       ↓
Authentication
       ↓
Organization Resolution
       ↓
Authorization / Entitlement
       ↓
Organization Data Resolver
       ↓
Correct Database / Storage
```

The Agent does not decide where organization data is stored.

---

# 16. Managed Data Flow

For:

```text
deploymentMode = MANAGED
```

the expected architecture is:

```text
Universal Agent
       ↓
OmniSight API
       ↓
Organization Resolver
       ↓
OmniSight Database
       +
OmniSight Storage
```

OmniSight controls:

* application hosting
* API hosting
* database
* storage
* infrastructure
* monitoring
* operational responsibility

---

# 17. Customer DB Data Flow

For:

```text
deploymentMode = CUSTOMER_DB
```

the expected architecture is:

```text
Universal Agent
       ↓
OmniSight API
       ↓
Organization Resolver
       ↓
Customer Database
       +
Customer Storage
```

OmniSight still hosts:

* application
* API
* control plane

Customer controls:

* primary organization database
* customer organization storage
* related infrastructure credentials
* database availability
* storage availability
* database backup
* storage operational policy

The Universal Agent remains unchanged.

---

# 18. Customer DB + Customer Storage Model

For the V1 Customer Database model, the architecture must treat customer database and customer storage as a coordinated customer-controlled data destination.

The model is conceptually:

```text
CUSTOMER_DB
    +
CUSTOMER_STORAGE
```

Customer Database stores appropriate organization metadata and structured data.

Customer Storage stores appropriate organization binary/file data.

Examples of binary data:

* Screenshots
* Collected media
* Other applicable monitoring files

The implementation must not assume that relational database storage and binary object storage are the same system.

---

# 19. Customer Database Responsibility

For Customer DB deployments, the customer is responsible for:

* Database availability
* Database infrastructure
* Database credentials
* Database backup policy
* Database operational health
* Database access control
* Database capacity
* Database maintenance according to the supported contract

OmniSight must securely access the customer database only through the configured server-side infrastructure integration.

Customer database credentials must never be exposed to:

* Browser
* Organization users
* Agent
* frontend JavaScript
* audit logs
* application logs
* API responses

unless a specific security architecture explicitly requires otherwise.

---

# 20. Customer Storage Responsibility

For Customer DB deployments, Customer Storage is the authoritative binary storage destination for organization-owned sensitive files where applicable.

The customer is responsible for:

* storage availability
* storage infrastructure
* storage credentials
* storage access policy
* storage capacity
* storage lifecycle/backup according to the supported contract

Storage credentials must remain server-side and securely managed.

They must never be delivered to the Universal Agent.

---

# 21. Server-Side Organization Resolver

OmniSight must have a server-side mechanism responsible for determining where organization data belongs.

Conceptually:

```text
Authenticated Request
        ↓
Organization Identity
        ↓
Organization Configuration
        ↓
Active Service Model
        ↓
Active Data Destination
        ↓
Database Resolver
        +
Storage Resolver
```

The resolver must be authoritative.

Clients and Agents must not choose arbitrary destinations.

---

# 22. Service Model Immutability

Every Organization has exactly one active service model.

V1 active values:

```text
MANAGED
CUSTOMER_DB
```

The service model must not be silently changed.

A service-model change is a controlled high-risk operation.

Any service-model change must:

1. Require explicit Super Admin authorization.
2. Validate compatibility.
3. Identify migration requirements.
4. Validate database requirements.
5. Validate storage requirements.
6. Validate Agent compatibility.
7. Validate subscription compatibility.
8. Record an audit event.
9. Require explicit confirmation.
10. Never occur as a side effect of an unrelated update.

An organization status update must never accidentally modify deployment mode.

---

# 23. Universal Agent and Service-Model Independence

Changing an organization's service model must not require a different Agent architecture.

The intended model is:

```text
Same Universal Agent
        ↓
OmniSight API
        ↓
Server-side destination resolution
        ↓
Managed OR Customer-controlled destination
```

The Agent should not need customer infrastructure credentials after a service-model change.

Where applicable, an Agent should continue operating after a successful server-side migration/cutover without requiring a new Agent build solely because the organization's database/storage destination changed.

---

# 24. Organization Infrastructure Configuration

Customer infrastructure configuration is server-side configuration.

It may include:

## Database

* Provider/type
* Host
* Port
* Database name
* Username
* Password/secret
* TLS/security settings
* Schema version
* Connection requirements

## Storage

* Provider/type
* Endpoint
* Bucket/container
* Region
* Access key
* Secret key
* TLS/security settings

Secrets must be encrypted at rest using appropriate secret-management practices.

---

# 25. Infrastructure Configuration Security

Customer infrastructure credentials must:

* never be stored plaintext where avoidable
* never be returned in normal API responses
* never be displayed in frontend forms after initial submission
* never be logged
* never be stored in audit logs
* never be included in Agent configuration
* never be embedded into Agent binaries
* never be committed to source control

The implementation should prefer a dedicated secret-management mechanism where practical.

---

# 26. Organization Infrastructure Change Request

An Organization Admin may request a change to the organization's customer database/storage destination.

The request does NOT immediately activate the new destination.

Expected flow:

```text
Organization Admin
        ↓
Submit Infrastructure Change
        ↓
Validation
        ↓
READY_FOR_APPROVAL
        ↓
Super Admin Review
        ↓
Approve / Reject
```

---

# 27. Infrastructure Change State Machine

The canonical state model is:

```text
CURRENT
   ↓
CHANGE_REQUESTED
   ↓
VALIDATING
   ↓
READY_FOR_APPROVAL
   ↓
APPROVED
   ↓
MIGRATING
   ↓
VERIFYING
   ↓
CUTOVER
   ↓
ACTIVE
```

Failure states must support safe recovery.

Possible failure path:

```text
VALIDATING
      ↓
VALIDATION_FAILED
      ↓
CURRENT
```

Migration failure:

```text
MIGRATING
      ↓
MIGRATION_FAILED
      ↓
ROLLBACK
      ↓
CURRENT
```

Verification failure:

```text
VERIFYING
      ↓
VERIFICATION_FAILED
      ↓
ROLLBACK
      ↓
CURRENT
```

---

# 28. Super Admin Infrastructure Approval

Only Super Admin may approve the activation of a new organization data destination.

Super Admin must be able to inspect:

* Current service model
* Current database destination
* Current storage destination
* Requested destination
* Validation results
* Schema compatibility
* Storage compatibility
* Migration readiness
* Risk information
* Migration status
* Verification results

Super Admin may:

```text
APPROVE
REJECT
```

Approval must be audited.

Rejection must be audited.

---

# 29. Infrastructure Validation

Before approval, the system must validate the requested destination.

Database validation may include:

* Connectivity
* Authentication
* Read access
* Write access
* Required permissions
* Database version
* Schema compatibility
* Required tables
* Required indexes
* Migration compatibility
* Transaction capability where required

Storage validation may include:

* Connectivity
* Authentication
* Bucket/container availability
* Write test
* Read test
* Permission validation
* Endpoint validation
* Region compatibility
* Object naming compatibility

Validation failures must prevent unsafe activation.

---

# 30. Migration Requirements

Changing from Managed to Customer DB must migrate existing organization data where applicable.

The system must NOT simply redirect future writes while leaving historical organization data behind unless an explicit business rule defines such behavior.

Migration may include:

* organization users
* memberships
* employees
* Agents/devices
* activity
* locations
* monitoring metadata
* screenshot metadata
* organization settings
* applicable historical records

Binary data may include:

* screenshots
* applicable collected files
* other organization-owned media

---

# 31. Migration of Metadata and Binary Data

The migration architecture must distinguish:

```text
Relational Metadata
```

from:

```text
Binary Files
```

Example:

```text
Screenshot Metadata
        ↓
Customer Database

Screenshot Binary
        ↓
Customer Storage
```

A migration is not considered complete merely because database records were copied.

Binary files must also be verified.

---

# 32. Migration Verification

Before cutover, migration verification must validate where applicable:

* record counts
* relationships
* organization ownership
* employee associations
* Agent associations
* activity records
* location records
* screenshot metadata
* binary files
* file sizes
* file hashes/checksums
* storage keys
* timestamps
* required indexes/constraints
* data integrity

The exact verification mechanism may evolve, but migration must provide evidence that the destination is usable.

---

# 33. Cutover

The new destination must not become authoritative until:

1. Destination validation succeeds.
2. Super Admin approves the migration.
3. Migration succeeds.
4. Verification succeeds.
5. Cutover is explicitly executed.

After cutover:

```text
Organization
      ↓
New Customer Database
      +
New Customer Storage
```

must become the authoritative destination for new organization data.

---

# 34. Rollback

Migration must support controlled rollback where technically feasible.

If migration or verification fails:

```text
New Destination
      ↓
Not Activated
```

The current working destination remains authoritative.

After cutover, rollback may be allowed during a defined safety window if the architecture supports it.

Rollback must be:

* explicit
* auditable
* controlled
* integrity-aware

---

# 35. Old Data Retention After Migration

Old organization data must not be immediately deleted after migration.

The previous destination should be retained for an appropriate verification/rollback period according to the defined operational policy.

Only after successful verification and the applicable retention/rollback window may old organization data be retired.

Data deletion must be auditable where required.

---

# 36. Migration Safety

Infrastructure migration is a high-risk operation.

The implementation should protect against:

* partial migration
* duplicate records
* missing records
* orphaned records
* missing binary files
* orphaned binary files
* concurrent writes
* inconsistent state
* race conditions
* interrupted migration
* retry duplication
* network failures
* database failures
* storage failures
* cutover failure

Migration operations should be idempotent where practical.

---

# 37. Package / Plan Model

Service Model and Package/Plan are separate concepts.

Example:

```text
Organization
├── Service Model: MANAGED
├── Package: PROFESSIONAL
├── Subscription: ACTIVE
├── Duration: 12 months
└── Payment: PAID
```

A package defines commercial/product entitlement.

A service model defines infrastructure/deployment/data-destination behavior.

These concepts must not be conflated.

---

# 38. Package Management

Super Admin must be able to manage packages/plans.

A package may contain:

* Name
* Description
* Price
* Billing duration
* Currency
* Included capabilities
* Limits
* Organization eligibility
* Active/inactive status
* Display order
* Landing-page visibility
* Commercial metadata

Package pricing must be configurable from Super Admin.

Hard-coded public pricing must not be required.

---

# 39. Subscription Model

Each Organization may have subscription information including:

* Package/Plan
* Service Model
* Subscription status
* Start date
* End date
* Duration
* Payment status
* Invoice information
* Renewal information
* Notes
* Audit history

Possible states include:

```text
PENDING
ACTIVE
PAUSED
EXPIRED
CANCELLED
```

The final enum may be refined during implementation, but state transitions must be explicit.

---

# 40. Manual Payment Model

OmniSight initially uses manual payment processing.

There is no requirement for an automated payment gateway in V1.

The payment flow is:

```text
Customer
   ↓
Contacts OmniSight
   ↓
Selects Package + Service Model
   ↓
Manual Payment
   ↓
Super Admin Verifies Payment
   ↓
Super Admin Records Payment
   ↓
Subscription Activated
   ↓
Organization Provisioned
```

Payment records must be auditable.

---

# 41. Subscription Expiration

Subscription expiration must be handled explicitly.

When an organization reaches its subscription end date:

* the organization becomes eligible for expiration handling
* the platform must not silently delete organization data
* access behavior follows subscription policy
* Super Admin can pause/suspend the organization
* Super Admin can renew/resume after payment

Data must be retained according to retention policy.

---

# 42. Organization Pause / Resume

Super Admin must be able to pause an organization.

Example:

```text
ACTIVE
  ↓
PAUSED
```

Pausing must not delete:

* Organization
* Users
* Employees
* Agents
* Screenshots
* Activity
* Historical records
* Payment history

unless a separate deletion operation is explicitly executed.

Resume:

```text
PAUSED
  ↓
ACTIVE
```

Resume must be audited.

---

# 43. Organization Provisioning

Organization creation is a Super Admin operation.

Preferred flow:

```text
Create Organization
       ↓
Select Service Model
       ↓
Select Package
       ↓
Set Subscription
       ↓
Record Payment / Invoice
       ↓
Create Organization Admin
       ↓
Create Membership
       ↓
Activate Organization
       ↓
Prepare Agent Provisioning
```

For CUSTOMER_DB organizations, customer infrastructure configuration may occur after Organization Admin login.

Provisioning must avoid partially-created organizations.

Where practical, database operations must be transactional.

---

# 44. Organization Admin Credentials

Super Admin provisions the initial Organization Admin account.

Passwords must never be stored in plaintext.

Credentials must follow secure authentication rules.

A secure first-login password-change flow is strongly preferred.

---

# 45. Universal Agent

The OmniSight Agent is the customer-side Universal service component.

There is exactly one canonical Agent architecture for V1.

The Agent must support both:

```text
MANAGED
CUSTOMER_DB
```

without requiring separate customer infrastructure credentials.

The Agent must respect:

* Organization identity
* Subscription state
* Package entitlement
* Enabled capabilities
* Server-side policy
* Device identity
* Security policy
* Agent compatibility

---

# 46. Agent Configuration

The Agent's foundational configuration should contain only OmniSight-centric information.

Examples:

```text
OmniSight API URL
Protocol Version
Agent Version
Secure Enrollment / Device Identity
```

The Agent must not receive:

```text
Customer DB URL
Customer DB username
Customer DB password
Customer Storage endpoint
Customer Storage access key
Customer Storage secret
```

---

# 47. Agent Organization Binding

An Agent must never be allowed to freely choose an organization at runtime.

Organization binding must be authentication-backed and server-authoritative.

The server must validate:

* Agent identity
* Organization identity
* Credential/token
* Device identity
* Subscription eligibility
* Service entitlement
* Agent compatibility
* Revocation state

Changing a local organization identifier must not allow an Agent to become another organization's Agent.

---

# 48. Agent Enrollment

The Agent must have a controlled enrollment/claiming mechanism.

Conceptually:

```text
Agent
   ↓
OmniSight API
   ↓
Enrollment Validation
   ↓
Organization Binding
   ↓
Device Registration
   ↓
Secure Credential
   ↓
Active Agent
```

Enrollment must prevent unauthorized device registration.

---

# 49. Agent Capability Model

Agent capabilities must be entitlement-aware.

Potential capabilities include:

* Screenshot
* Location
* Activity monitoring
* Device information
* Application inventory
* Remote commands
* Other approved monitoring capabilities

Capabilities must not be enabled solely because a local UI toggle exists.

The server remains authoritative.

---

# 50. Build-Time vs Runtime Entitlement

OmniSight uses a hybrid model.

## Build-Time

Agent Builder may package configuration according to:

* Organization
* Service Model
* Package
* Required capabilities
* Initial configuration

## Runtime

The server remains authoritative.

The Agent must respect applicable:

* Organization status
* Subscription status
* Package entitlement
* Capability policy
* Security state
* Revocation
* Compatibility requirements

Modifying a local Agent package must not grant unauthorized server capabilities.

---

# 51. Agent Builder

The Agent Builder is a Super Admin/local administrative tool.

It must allow Super Admin to:

1. Select Organization.
2. Validate Organization status.
3. Validate Package.
4. Validate Service Model.
5. Determine permitted capabilities.
6. Generate organization-bound Agent configuration where applicable.
7. Build the Universal Agent.
8. Validate build output.
9. Verify package integrity.
10. Export deliverable.

The Builder must not generate separate Managed/Customer Agent architectures.

The Builder must not embed customer database/storage secrets.

The Builder must fail safely when configuration is invalid.

---

# 52. Agent Runtime Configuration

Runtime configuration must be server-authoritative.

The Agent may receive:

* monitoring policy
* capability configuration
* screenshot interval
* location policy
* command policy
* update policy
* retention-related local behavior
* server endpoint information
* protocol requirements

The Agent must not use local configuration to bypass server policy.

---

# 53. Agent Heartbeat

Agents must periodically communicate with the server.

Heartbeat information may include:

* Device identity
* Agent version
* Organization identity
* Timestamp
* Health status
* Capabilities
* Relevant configuration state
* Protocol version

Offline detection must be server-driven.

---

# 54. Agent Status

Typical states:

```text
ONLINE
OFFLINE
UNKNOWN
DISABLED
REVOKED
```

Status must be derived from server-observed signals such as heartbeat/connection state.

Client UI assumptions must not be treated as authoritative device status.

---

# 55. Agent Revocation

Super Admin or authorized server-side controls must support Agent revocation.

A revoked Agent must not continue using protected OmniSight capabilities.

Revocation must be server-enforced.

Revocation must be audited.

---

# 56. Agent Offline Behavior

The Agent may temporarily operate offline according to server-defined policy.

If local data is queued:

* it should be protected using appropriate local encryption
* local retention must be policy-controlled
* upload must resume when connectivity returns
* duplicate uploads must be prevented
* unauthorized offline operation must not bypass entitlement policy

---

# 57. Agent Security

Agent security must include where applicable:

* secure transport
* secure authentication
* credential protection
* local secret protection
* local data encryption
* integrity verification
* version verification
* revocation
* secure update mechanism
* safe logging
* crash handling

Agent logs must not expose customer infrastructure credentials.

---

# 58. Web ↔ Agent Contract

The Web/control plane and Agent must share a documented contract.

The contract must define:

* Authentication
* Enrollment/claiming
* Organization binding
* Device identity
* Heartbeat
* Configuration
* Capabilities
* Screenshot upload
* Location
* Commands
* Version compatibility
* Error codes
* Revocation
* Subscription state
* Policy enforcement

Any breaking contract change must be coordinated across:

```text
omnisight-web
+
omnisight-agent
```

---

# 59. Server-Side Agent Authorization

Every Agent API request must validate as applicable:

```text
Authentication
      ↓
Agent Identity
      ↓
Organization Binding
      ↓
Agent Status
      ↓
Organization Status
      ↓
Subscription
      ↓
Entitlement
      ↓
Capability Policy
      ↓
Resource Authorization
```

The Agent must never be treated as a trusted authority.

---

# 60. Screenshot Pipeline

Expected architecture:

```text
Agent
 ↓
OmniSight API
 ↓
Authentication
 ↓
Organization Resolution
 ↓
Entitlement / Policy Check
 ↓
Screenshot Metadata
 ↓
Correct Organization Database

Screenshot Binary
 ↓
Correct Organization Storage
```

For Managed:

```text
Metadata → OmniSight DB
Binary   → OmniSight Storage
```

For Customer DB:

```text
Metadata → Customer DB
Binary   → Customer Storage
```

---

# 61. Screenshot Metadata

Screenshot metadata may include:

* Screenshot ID
* Organization ID
* Employee ID
* Device ID
* Captured timestamp
* Storage key
* File size
* Hash/checksum
* Status
* Applicable policy metadata

The exact schema may evolve.

---

# 62. Screenshot Collection Policy

Screenshot collection must be governed by:

* Organization entitlement
* Subscription
* Service model
* Screenshot policy
* Privacy/consent policy
* Collection interval
* Agent status
* Storage policy

Server-side policy must be authoritative.

The Agent must not arbitrarily choose a frequency that violates server policy.

---

# 63. Screenshot Retention

Screenshot retention must be configurable according to applicable subscription/service policy.

The system should support:

* Retention duration
* Automatic cleanup
* Storage monitoring
* Storage usage reporting
* Safe deletion

Deletion must be auditable where required.

---

# 64. Storage Architecture

Monitoring products can generate large amounts of binary data.

OmniSight must distinguish:

```text
Metadata
```

from:

```text
Binary Data
```

Relational databases should primarily store metadata and structured operational data.

Object/file storage should be used for large binary files where appropriate.

---

# 65. Storage Access Security

Organization storage access must be authorized server-side.

The system must prevent:

* cross-tenant file access
* guessed storage-key access
* unauthorized downloads
* unauthorized deletion
* unauthorized public access

Signed URLs or equivalent controlled access mechanisms may be used where appropriate.

Storage authorization must remain tenant-aware.

---

# 66. Live Activity

Live Activity is an operational feature.

It must provide near-real-time visibility where supported.

The system must enforce:

* Authentication
* Organization isolation
* Role permissions
* Agent/device authorization
* Connection security

Real-time channels must never allow cross-organization subscription.

---

# 67. Organization Settings

Organization users should be able to view:

* Organization name
* Organization status
* Service Model
* Package
* Subscription duration
* Subscription start date
* Subscription end date
* Subscription status
* Relevant limits
* Relevant enabled capabilities

Super Admin-owned commercial/deployment fields must be read-only for Organization Admin.

Organization Admin may have a dedicated:

```text
Data Infrastructure
```

area for submitting customer DB/storage configuration.

---

# 68. Customer Infrastructure UI

Organization Admin may access:

```text
Settings
    ↓
Data Infrastructure
```

The UI may show:

* Current Service Model
* Current data destination status
* Customer DB status
* Customer Storage status
* Configuration status
* Validation status
* Migration status
* Approval status

Sensitive credentials must not be displayed after submission.

---

# 69. Infrastructure Request UX

The infrastructure change UI must clearly communicate:

```text
Submitting configuration does NOT immediately switch the organization's data destination.
```

The UI should show:

```text
Current
Requested
Validation
Approval
Migration
Verification
Cutover
```

This prevents users from assuming that entering credentials immediately changes production routing.

---

# 70. Super Admin Settings

Super Admin settings may include:

## Business

* Company/platform information
* Contact information
* Support information

## Pricing

* Package prices
* Package availability
* Package descriptions

## Landing Page

* Hero content
* Product description
* Pricing
* Features
* Contact information
* CTA configuration
* Business information

## Branding

* Logo
* Brand name
* Colors
* Assets
* Other supported branding configuration

Changes must be validated and audited where appropriate.

---

# 71. Landing Page

OmniSight must provide a public marketing landing page.

The landing page must communicate clearly:

* What OmniSight is
* Who it is for
* Key capabilities
* Service models
* Packages/pricing
* How to purchase
* How manual payment works
* How to contact OmniSight

V1 customer-facing service models are exactly:

```text
OmniSight Managed
Customer Database
```

Private deployment must not be shown as a V1 option.

---

# 72. Purchase Flow

The public customer journey is:

```text
Landing Page
    ↓
Explore OmniSight
    ↓
Choose Package
    ↓
Choose Service Model
    ↓
Contact OmniSight
    ↓
Manual Payment
    ↓
Super Admin Verification
    ↓
Organization Provisioning
```

The initial implementation does not require automated checkout.

---

# 73. Service Model Selection UI

When provisioning an organization, Super Admin must see exactly two V1 options.

### OmniSight Managed

> OmniSight hosts the application, database and storage. Best for customers who want a fully managed OmniSight service.

### Customer Database

> OmniSight hosts the application/API. The customer controls the organization's primary database and customer storage. Customer infrastructure is configured securely and becomes active only after validation, Super Admin approval, migration where applicable, verification, and controlled cutover.

Private deployment must not be shown, selectable, or offered in V1.

---

# 74. Authorization

Authorization must be enforced server-side.

The UI may hide unavailable actions, but hiding a button is not authorization.

Every privileged API route must independently verify:

1. Authentication
2. Role
3. Organization context
4. Resource ownership
5. Permission
6. Subscription eligibility where applicable
7. Service-model eligibility where applicable

---

# 75. RBAC

The canonical role hierarchy is:

```text
SUPER_ADMIN

ORGANIZATION
├── ORGANIZATION_ADMIN
├── MANAGER
├── VIEWER
└── EMPLOYEE
```

Role names must be centralized.

Permission logic must not be duplicated inconsistently across pages.

---

# 76. Audit Logging

Important operations must generate audit records.

Examples:

* Organization created
* Organization updated
* Service Model selected
* Service Model changed
* Package changed
* Subscription created
* Subscription renewed
* Subscription paused
* Subscription resumed
* Payment recorded
* Invoice created
* Organization Admin created
* User created
* User role changed
* User removed
* Agent issued
* Agent revoked
* Infrastructure change requested
* Infrastructure validation completed
* Infrastructure change approved
* Infrastructure change rejected
* Migration started
* Migration completed
* Migration failed
* Cutover completed
* Rollback executed
* Security-sensitive settings changed

Audit logs must identify:

* Actor
* Action
* Target
* Organization context
* Timestamp
* Relevant metadata
* Result/status

Secrets must never be stored inside audit logs.

---

# 77. Data Isolation

Every organization-scoped resource must be evaluated for tenant isolation.

Where applicable:

```text
organizationId
```

must be present and validated.

The implementation must distinguish:

```text
Platform Scope
Organization Scope
User Scope
Device Scope
```

Global unique constraints must only be used when the business rule truly requires global uniqueness.

---

# 78. Database Requirements

The platform must support data required for:

* Organizations
* Users
* Roles
* Memberships
* Employees
* Agents/devices
* Packages/plans
* Subscriptions
* Payments
* Invoices
* Audit logs
* Organization settings
* Platform settings
* Monitoring data
* Screenshot metadata
* Activity data
* Location data where applicable
* Agent configuration metadata
* Infrastructure configuration metadata
* Migration state where applicable

For CUSTOMER_DB organizations, organization-owned data must be routed to the configured customer database according to the approved data architecture.

---

# 79. Infrastructure Configuration Data

Infrastructure configuration records must identify, where applicable:

* Organization
* Service Model
* Database configuration reference
* Storage configuration reference
* Configuration version
* Validation status
* Approval status
* Migration status
* Active/inactive state
* Created timestamp
* Updated timestamp

Sensitive credentials must be stored through secure secret handling.

---

# 80. API Requirements

APIs must be organized around clear boundaries.

Examples:

```text
/api/admin/*
/api/organization/*
/api/agent/*
/api/auth/*
/api/public/*
```

Every API must have:

* Authentication requirement
* Authorization requirement
* Input validation
* Output contract
* Error contract
* Tenant boundary
* Audit requirement where applicable
* Rate limiting where applicable

Agent APIs must additionally enforce:

* Agent authentication
* Organization binding
* Agent status
* Subscription eligibility
* Capability entitlement
* Server-side policy

---

# 81. Organization Data Routing

The API layer must resolve organization data destination server-side.

Conceptually:

```text
Request
  ↓
Authenticated Identity
  ↓
Organization
  ↓
Active Service Model
  ↓
Active Destination
  ↓
Database Resolver / Storage Resolver
  ↓
Data Operation
```

No client-side implementation may override the active destination.

---

# 82. Error Handling

Every production-critical page and API flow must provide meaningful error states.

The UI must support where applicable:

```text
Loading
Success
Empty
Error
Retry
Unauthorized
Forbidden
Expired
Paused
Unavailable
Migration Pending
Migration Failed
Validation Failed
Approval Pending
```

API failures must produce structured responses.

Infrastructure failures must not expose secrets or raw sensitive connection information.

---

# 83. Subscription-Aware Access

Access to paid functionality must consider:

```text
Authentication
      ↓
Role
      ↓
Organization
      ↓
Organization Status
      ↓
Subscription Status
      ↓
Package Entitlement
      ↓
Service Model
      ↓
Feature Policy
```

A user must not gain access merely because a frontend route exists.

---

# 84. Deployment Model and Feature Policy

V1 active models:

```text
MANAGED
    → OmniSight application
    → OmniSight API
    → OmniSight database
    → OmniSight storage

CUSTOMER_DB
    → OmniSight application
    → OmniSight API
    → Customer database
    → Customer storage
```

Private is not a V1 model.

---

# 85. Customer DB Operational Boundary

For CUSTOMER_DB deployments, the production contract must explicitly define:

* Which database OmniSight can access
* Which storage OmniSight can access
* How credentials are configured
* How secrets are protected
* Backup responsibility
* Migration responsibility
* Schema versioning
* Storage compatibility
* Database connectivity requirements
* Storage connectivity requirements
* Failure behavior
* Support boundary
* Monitoring responsibility
* Retention responsibility
* Rollback requirements

These requirements must be documented before production rollout.

---

# 86. Organization Lifecycle

Canonical lifecycle:

```text
PROVISIONING
     ↓
ACTIVE
     ↓
PAUSED
     ↓
ACTIVE
     ↓
EXPIRED
     ↓
RENEWED / ACTIVE
```

Service-model migration is a separate controlled lifecycle.

Deletion is a separate destructive workflow.

---

# 87. Organization Deletion

Deletion is a high-risk operation.

The system must distinguish:

### Directly deletable

Resources with no dependent records may be deleted directly.

### Dependency-bound

Resources with dependencies must first have those dependencies handled.

The system should warn Super Admin before destructive actions.

No destructive cascade should occur silently.

Customer infrastructure data must not be deleted merely because an organization is paused.

---

# 88. Seed Data

Production seed data must be minimal.

Only required platform bootstrap data should remain.

Super Admin bootstrap data should come from secure environment/configuration where applicable.

Customer organizations must not be created as permanent demo seed data unless explicitly required for development/testing.

Development test data must not accidentally ship into production.

---

# 89. Email System

Initial OmniSight sales and provisioning may use manual communication.

Automated transactional email is not required for the initial manual-payment workflow.

A future email system may support:

* Organization created
* Welcome email
* Password setup
* Subscription expiring
* Subscription expired
* Payment confirmation
* Invoice
* Agent instructions
* Security notifications
* Migration notifications

A mock email compose implementation must not be represented as actual email delivery.

---

# 90. Web Repository Scope

Repository:

```text
E:\Live project\omnisight\omnisight-web
```

The Web repository is responsible for:

* Super Admin Panel
* Organization Admin Panel
* Organization user interfaces
* Authentication
* Authorization
* API/control plane
* Database integration
* Customer infrastructure integration
* Subscription management
* Payment/invoice management
* Platform configuration
* Landing page
* Branding
* Audit UI
* Agent management UI
* Monitoring UI
* Real-time UI
* Migration management
* Infrastructure validation
* Infrastructure approval workflow

---

# 91. Agent Repository Scope

Repository:

```text
E:\Live project\omnisight\omnisight-agent
```

The Agent repository is responsible for:

* Universal Agent runtime
* Device identity
* Server communication
* Enrollment
* Heartbeat
* Capability execution
* Monitoring collection
* Policy enforcement
* Secure authentication
* Configuration
* Upload queue
* Offline behavior
* Agent lifecycle
* Build/package generation
* Agent security
* Update/version management

The Agent must never independently redefine business entitlement.

Server-side authorization remains authoritative.

---

# 92. Version Compatibility

The server must know the Agent version.

The Agent should report:

```text
agentVersion
protocolVersion
capabilities
deviceId
organizationId
```

The server must be able to identify unsupported Agent versions.

Agent upgrades must not silently break customer environments.

---

# 93. Observability

The platform must provide sufficient observability for:

* API errors
* Authentication failures
* Agent connection failures
* Database failures
* Storage failures
* Subscription errors
* Provisioning failures
* Agent build failures
* Real-time connection failures
* Infrastructure validation failures
* Migration failures
* Cutover failures
* Rollback failures

Sensitive credentials and private data must not be logged.

---

# 94. Performance and Scalability

OmniSight may process large volumes of:

* Screenshots
* Activity events
* Device heartbeats
* Location data
* Audit records
* Migration data

The architecture must avoid unnecessary database growth.

Where applicable, use:

* Pagination
* Indexing
* Background processing
* Object storage
* Retention cleanup
* Aggregation
* Caching
* Efficient queries
* Queue-based processing

Unbounded list endpoints are prohibited for production-scale data.

---

# 95. Pagination

Large collections must use server-side pagination.

Examples:

* Organizations
* Members
* Employees
* Agents
* Screenshots
* Activity
* Locations
* Audit logs
* Payments
* Invoices
* Migration history

The backend must enforce safe maximum page sizes.

---

# 96. Privacy

Monitoring functionality must respect applicable privacy and consent requirements.

The product must support policy-driven control over monitoring features.

Examples:

* Screenshot enabled/disabled
* Location enabled/disabled
* Monitoring enabled/disabled
* Privacy/break mode where applicable

The system must not silently collect data when applicable policy prohibits collection.

---

# 97. Data Retention

Retention must be defined separately for:

* Screenshots
* Activity
* Location
* Audit logs
* Device records
* Payment records
* Invoices
* Subscription history
* Migration artifacts
* Temporary migration backups

Financial/audit records generally require stronger retention guarantees than transient monitoring data.

Retention policies must be documented and implemented explicitly.

---

# 98. Super Admin Dashboard

The Super Admin dashboard should provide high-level operational visibility.

Potential information:

* Total organizations
* Active organizations
* Paused organizations
* Expiring subscriptions
* Expired subscriptions
* Revenue/payment summary
* Active Agents
* Offline Agents
* Storage usage
* Screenshot volume
* Recent audit events
* Platform health
* Infrastructure validation status
* Migration status
* Customer database health where observable
* Customer storage health where observable

Dashboard metrics must be database-driven.

No random/mock production metrics are permitted.

---

# 99. Organization Dashboard

Organization users should see only their organization's data.

Dashboard may include:

* Employee count
* Active Agents
* Online/offline devices
* Activity summary
* Screenshot summary
* Location summary where enabled
* Subscription status
* Package
* Service Model
* Alerts
* Operational metrics

---

# 100. UI/UX Requirements

The UI must clearly distinguish:

* Platform administration
* Organization administration
* Operational monitoring
* Subscription information
* Service Model
* Package
* Customer infrastructure
* Migration state

Critical actions must have:

* Confirmation
* Clear consequences
* Permission checks
* Error feedback
* Success feedback

Infrastructure migration and destructive actions require additional confirmation.

---

# 101. No Silent State Changes

The following must never happen silently:

* Service Model changes
* Package changes
* Subscription status changes
* Organization status changes
* Role changes
* Agent revocation
* Data deletion
* Storage deletion
* Security setting changes
* Infrastructure changes
* Migration
* Cutover
* Rollback

Every important state transition must be intentional and auditable.

---

# 102. Source-of-Truth Rules

When implementing OmniSight, follow this priority:

```text
1. Master PRD
2. Feature-specific PRD
3. Web ↔ Agent Contract
4. Database Contract
5. Infrastructure/Data Destination Contract
6. Security Rules
7. Existing Implementation
8. AI/Developer Assumption
```

If two requirements conflict, the higher-level authoritative requirement wins.

If the PRD itself is ambiguous, the developer/AI must not invent behavior silently.

---

# 103. AI Coding Agent Rules

Any AI coding agent working on OmniSight must follow these rules.

## Rule 1 — Do Not Guess

Never invent:

* Business rules
* Permissions
* Subscription behavior
* Service model behavior
* Database ownership
* Storage ownership
* API contracts
* Agent capabilities
* Migration behavior

when the requirement is undefined.

## Rule 2 — Inspect Before Editing

Before modifying code:

1. Inspect relevant files.
2. Inspect database schema.
3. Inspect existing API.
4. Inspect authentication.
5. Inspect authorization.
6. Inspect related UI.
7. Inspect Agent contract.
8. Inspect infrastructure routing.
9. Inspect migrations.
10. Identify dependencies.

## Rule 3 — Preserve Existing Functionality

Do not break unrelated working functionality.

## Rule 4 — Security First

Never weaken:

* RBAC
* tenant isolation
* authentication
* CSRF
* rate limiting
* validation
* audit controls
* Agent authentication

to make a feature easier to implement.

## Rule 5 — No Client-Only Security

A client-side permission check is never sufficient.

## Rule 6 — No Mock Production Data

Production functionality must use real data.

Do not use:

```text
Math.random()
fake arrays
placeholder production records
hardcoded organization data
fake payment data
fake Agent status
```

unless explicitly part of a development/test environment.

## Rule 7 — Database Changes Require Impact Analysis

Before changing schema:

* Identify dependencies.
* Identify migrations.
* Identify existing data.
* Identify indexes.
* Identify constraints.
* Identify API impact.
* Identify Agent impact.
* Identify tenant-isolation impact.
* Identify migration impact.

## Rule 8 — API Changes Require Contract Review

Any change to an API used by the Agent requires Web ↔ Agent contract review.

## Rule 9 — Destructive Operations Must Be Safe

Never introduce silent destructive cascades without explicit requirement.

## Rule 10 — Customer Infrastructure Secrets

Never place customer database/storage secrets inside:

* Agent
* frontend
* browser
* public API responses
* source code
* logs
* audit records
* Agent Builder output

## Rule 11 — Verify

After implementation, run applicable:

* Typecheck
* Lint
* Unit tests
* Integration tests
* Build
* API tests
* Agent tests
* Security checks
* Migration checks

---

# 104. Definition of Done

A feature is not complete merely because its UI exists.

A feature is complete only when applicable layers are implemented:

```text
UI
 ↓
Client logic
 ↓
API
 ↓
Authentication
 ↓
Authorization
 ↓
Tenant isolation
 ↓
Business logic
 ↓
Database
 ↓
Storage where applicable
 ↓
Audit
 ↓
Error handling
 ↓
Tests
```

For infrastructure features:

```text
UI
 ↓
API
 ↓
Authentication
 ↓
Authorization
 ↓
Secret Management
 ↓
Validation
 ↓
Approval
 ↓
Migration
 ↓
Verification
 ↓
Cutover
 ↓
Rollback handling
 ↓
Audit
 ↓
Tests
```

For Agent-related features:

```text
Web
 ↓
API Contract
 ↓
Authentication
 ↓
Entitlement
 ↓
Organization Binding
 ↓
Universal Agent
 ↓
Runtime behavior
 ↓
Server verification
 ↓
Tests
```

---

# 105. Testing Requirements

Testing must cover:

## Authentication

* Valid login
* Invalid login
* Expired session
* Logout
* Rate limiting

## Authorization

* Super Admin access
* Organization Admin access
* Manager access
* Viewer access
* Employee access
* Forbidden access

## Tenant Isolation

* Organization A cannot access Organization B
* Cross-tenant API manipulation fails
* Cross-tenant Agent access fails
* Cross-tenant storage access fails
* Cross-tenant audit access fails

## Subscription

* Active
* Paused
* Expired
* Renewal
* Payment recording

## Service Models

* Managed
* Customer DB
* Invalid transitions
* Unauthorized changes
* Super Admin approval
* Customer infrastructure validation

## Infrastructure

* Database connectivity validation
* Storage connectivity validation
* Invalid credentials
* Permission failures
* Migration failure
* Verification failure
* Cutover
* Rollback

## Agent

* Authentication
* Enrollment
* Organization binding
* Heartbeat
* Capability policy
* Revocation
* Subscription enforcement
* Version compatibility
* Offline behavior

## Data

* Pagination
* Retention
* Storage
* Audit
* Migration integrity
* Screenshot metadata
* Screenshot binary integrity

---

